1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:02,639
the pigeons said.

2
00:00:02,639 --> 00:00:08,039
None of the animals ever mentioned Molly again.

3
00:00:08,039 --> 00:00:11,559
In January, there came bitterly hard weather.

4
00:00:11,559 --> 00:00:16,199
The earth was like iron, and nothing could be done in the fields.

5
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:20,840
Many meetings were held in the big barn, and the pigs occupied themselves with

6
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:24,039
planning out the work of the coming season.

7
00:00:24,039 --> 00:00:28,679
It had come to be accepted that the pigs, who were manifestly cleverer than

8
00:00:28,679 --> 00:00:35,239
the other animals, should decide all questions of farm policy, though their decisions had to be

9
00:00:35,239 --> 00:00:38,199
ratified by a majority vote.

10
00:00:38,199 --> 00:00:42,400
This arrangement would have worked well enough if it had not been for the

11
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:46,280
disputes between Snowball and Napoleon.

12
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:51,719
These two disagreed at every point where disagreement was possible.

13
00:00:51,719 --> 00:00:56,798
If one of them suggested sowing a bigger acreage with barley, the other was

14
00:00:56,798 --> 00:01:02,159
certain to demand a bigger acreage of oats. And if one of them said that such

15
00:01:02,159 --> 00:01:06,798
and such a field was just right for cabbages, the other would declare that

16
00:01:06,798 --> 00:01:10,560
it was useless for anything except roots.

17
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:16,119
Each had his own following, and there were some violent debates.

18
00:01:16,119 --> 00:01:21,960
At the meetings, Snowball often won over the majority by his brilliant speeches,

19
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:27,359
but Napoleon was better at canvassing support for himself in between times.

20
00:01:27,359 --> 00:01:30,880
He was especially successful with the sheep.

21
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:37,439
Of late, the sheep had taken to bleating "Four legs good, two legs bad."

22
00:01:37,439 --> 00:01:43,039
both in and out of season, and they often interrupted the meeting with this.

23
00:01:43,039 --> 00:01:50,439
It was noticed that they were especially liable to break into Four legs good, two legs bad.

24
00:01:50,439 --> 00:01:54,439
at crucial moments in Snowball's speeches.

25
00:01:54,439 --> 00:01:58,920
Snowball had made a close study of some back numbers of the Farmer and Stock

26
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:03,159
Breeder, which he had found in the farmhouse, and was full of plans for

27
00:02:03,159 --> 00:02:06,119
innovations and improvements.

28
00:02:06,119 --> 00:02:12,838
He talked learnedly about field drains, silage, and basic slag, and had worked

29
00:02:12,838 --> 00:02:17,239
out a complicated scheme for all the animals to drop their dung directly in

30
00:02:17,239 --> 00:02:24,000
the fields at a different spot every day to save the labor of cartage.

31
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,079
Napoleon produced no schemes of his own, but said quietly that Snowball's would come to nothing,

32
00:02:31,079 --> 00:02:34,399
and seemed to be biding his time.

33
00:02:34,399 --> 00:02:43,039
But of all their controversies, none was so bitter as the one that took place over the windmill.

34
00:02:43,359 --> 00:02:48,279
In the long pasture, not far from the farm buildings, there was a small knoll,

35
00:02:48,279 --> 00:02:51,359
which was the highest point on the farm.

36
00:02:51,359 --> 00:02:57,399
After surveying the ground, Snowball declared that this was just the place for a windmill,

37
00:02:57,399 --> 00:03:03,439
which could be made to operate a dynamo and supply the farm with electrical power.

38
00:03:03,439 --> 00:03:08,119
This would light the stalls and warm them in winter, and would also run a

39
00:03:08,119 --> 00:03:15,680
circular saw, a chaff cutter, a mangel slicer, and an electric milking machine.

40
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:19,800
The animals had never heard of anything of this kind before,

41
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:25,439
for the farm was an old-fashioned one and had only the most primitive machinery.

42
00:03:25,439 --> 00:03:30,039
And they listened in astonishment while Snowball conjured up pictures of

43
00:03:30,039 --> 00:03:35,039
fantastic machines which would do their work for them while they grazed at their

44
00:03:35,039 --> 00:03:40,720
ease in the fields or improved their minds with reading and conversation.

45
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:45,880
Within a few weeks, Snowball's plans for the windmill were fully worked out.

46
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:50,640
The mechanical details came mostly from three books, which had belonged to Mr.

47
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:52,079
Jones.

48
00:03:52,079 --> 00:03:58,199
1,000 useful things to do about the house, Every Man His Own Bricklayer,

49
00:03:58,199 --> 00:04:01,880
and Electricity for Beginners.

50
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:07,439
Snowball used as his study a shed, which had once been used for incubators and

51
00:04:07,439 --> 00:04:11,479
had a smooth wooden floor suitable for drawing on.

52
00:04:11,479 --> 00:04:17,960
He was closeted there for hours at a time, with his books held open by a stone and

53
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:22,000
with a piece of chalk gripped between the knuckles of his trotter, he would

54
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:30,359
move rapidly to and fro, drawing in line after line and uttering little whimpers of excitement.

55
00:04:30,359 --> 00:04:34,920
Gradually, the plans grew into a complicated mass of cranks and

56
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:40,119
cogwheels, covering more than half the floor, which the other animals found

57
00:04:40,119 --> 00:04:44,800
completely unintelligible but very impressive.

58
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:49,439
All of them came to look at Snowball's drawings at least once a day.

59
00:04:49,439 --> 00:04:55,720
Even the hens and ducks came and were at pains not to tread on the chalk marks.

60
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:58,720
Only Napoleon held aloof.

61
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:03,000
He had declared himself against the windmill from the start.

62
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,439
One day, however, he arrived unexpectedly to examine the plans.

63
00:05:08,439 --> 00:05:14,279
He walked heavily round the shed, looked closely at every detail of the plans,

64
00:05:14,279 --> 00:05:18,840
and snuffed at them once or twice, then stood for a little while

65
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:22,119
contemplating them out of the corner of his eye.

66
00:05:22,119 --> 00:05:28,439
Then, suddenly, he lifted his leg, urinated over the plans, and walked out

67
00:05:28,439 --> 00:05:30,880
without uttering a word.

68
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:35,279
The whole farm was deeply divided on the subject of the windmill.

69
00:05:35,279 --> 00:05:39,359
Snowball did not deny that to build it would be a difficult business.

70
00:05:39,359 --> 00:05:43,600
Stone would have to be quarried and built up into walls, then the sails

71
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:48,720
would have to be made, and after that, there would be need for dynamos and cables.

72
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:52,920
How these were to be procured, Snowball did not say.

73
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:56,359
But he maintained that it could all be done in a year.

74
00:05:56,359 --> 00:06:00,720
And thereafter, he declared so much labor would be saved that the animals

75
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:04,079
would only need to work 3 days a week.

76
00:06:04,079 --> 00:06:08,399
Napoleon, on the other hand, argued that the great need of the moment was to

77
00:06:08,399 --> 00:06:12,960
increase food production and that if they wasted time on the windmill, they

78
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:15,399
would all starve to death.

79
00:06:15,399 --> 00:06:19,920
The animals formed themselves into two factions under the slogans,

80
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:23,159
>> "Vote for Snowball and the 3-day week."

81
00:06:23,159 --> 00:06:27,600
>> and >> "Vote for Napoleon and the full manger."

82
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:32,600
>> Benjamin was the only animal who did not side with either faction.

83
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:37,159
He refused to believe either that food would become more plentiful or that the

84
00:06:37,159 --> 00:06:39,479
windmill would save work.

85
00:06:39,479 --> 00:06:41,000
"Windmill or no windmill," he said,
